1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,232
Clinical communication changes what happens next. A patient who
understands the plan can participate; a receiving clinician who
understands a trend can set the right priority.

2
00:00:11,232 --> 00:00:23,376
Communication can fail even when every individual sentence is true. A
report may contain an old blood pressure but omit the newer, lower
reading that changes the clinical concern.

3
00:00:23,376 --> 00:00:36,528
A useful report connects the problem, relevant findings, actions,
response and remaining need. Accuracy includes chronology and
uncertainty, not just a list of correct facts.

4
00:00:36,528 --> 00:00:47,064
During this lesson, practise speaking aloud. Knowing what each letter
means is only the first step; the skill is choosing and delivering the
information another person needs.

5
00:00:47,064 --> 00:00:58,080
Patient explanations should use familiar words and short, concrete
statements. Say what you are doing, why it matters and what the person
may feel or need to do.

6
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:09,456
A professional report can use precise clinical terms, measured values
and times. Replace vague phrases such as vitals okay with the findings
that support the assessment.

7
00:01:09,456 --> 00:01:22,704
An acronym is useful only if the listener understands it. Expand an
unfamiliar abbreviation, especially when handing over medications, an
unusual device or an uncertain finding.

8
00:01:22,704 --> 00:01:32,184
Match the message to the listener without changing its meaning. Plain
language should improve access, not conceal risk or offer false
reassurance.

9
00:01:32,184 --> 00:01:44,280
Teach-back asks the patient to explain important information in their
own words. It checks your explanation and the plan's usability, rather
than testing whether the patient can repeat a sentence.

10
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:53,352
An example is: I want to make sure I explained this clearly. What will
happen next, and what would you tell us if you started to feel worse?

11
00:01:53,352 --> 00:02:03,192
If the answer reveals a misunderstanding, explain the missed point
differently and ask again. A yes to do you understand gives much less
information.

12
00:02:03,192 --> 00:02:13,200
Use appropriate language, hearing and cognitive support. Teach-back
helps assess understanding, but it is not by itself a full capacity
assessment.

13
00:02:13,200 --> 00:02:22,896
An open question lets the patient tell you what they think matters.
Focused questions then clarify timing, relevant symptoms and immediate
risks.

14
00:02:22,896 --> 00:02:32,544
If a patient says I blacked out, do not assume a specific event. Ask
what they remember, whether someone witnessed it and what the witness
observed.

15
00:02:32,544 --> 00:02:43,488
Summarize the account and invite correction: the dizziness began while
standing, you sat down, and you are unsure whether you lost
consciousness. Is that accurate?

16
00:02:43,488 --> 00:02:55,488
Separate the patient's words from your interpretation in both handover
and documentation. This preserves uncertainty rather than turning an
unclear symptom into an established diagnosis.

17
00:02:55,488 --> 00:03:06,792
The AET module teaches radio patches, SBAR and MIST. You need to
understand those school competencies and also recognize the current
service guidance for their use.

18
00:03:06,792 --> 00:03:19,896
BCEHS A03 recommends ATMIST AMBO for transfer of care in all settings,
and SBAR for consultations. It states that using SBAR for other
transfers of care is deprecated.

19
00:03:19,896 --> 00:03:31,032
MIST remains useful to understand the school's trauma-summary task. In
current BCEHS transfer practice, the fuller framework adds identity,
time and relevant background.

20
00:03:31,032 --> 00:03:42,960
Choose by purpose rather than labelling all medical patients SBAR and
all trauma patients MIST. A service instruction and an acronym
exercise are different contexts.

21
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:54,864
A pre-arrival patch gives the receiving team time to prepare. Identify
your unit, the intended resource and the estimated arrival, then state
the main problem and level of concern.

22
00:03:54,864 --> 00:04:05,088
Select the findings that explain urgency. Include meaningful trends,
relevant treatment and response, not the complete history in the order
you obtained it.

23
00:04:05,088 --> 00:04:18,240
State what you need: a receiving clinician, a specific preparation or
further clinical consultation through the appropriate route. A long
report without a clear request may leave the listener unsure what to
do.

24
00:04:18,240 --> 00:04:28,584
Use the service's pre-alert criteria and approved format. A radio
patch does not replace the bedside transfer report or confirmation
that care has been accepted.

25
00:04:28,584 --> 00:04:37,152
In a consultation, start with the reason for contacting the clinician
now. State your role and patient context, then the immediate concern.

26
00:04:37,152 --> 00:04:47,736
Background should be selective. Include information that changes
interpretation or the requested decision, rather than reading every
item from the patient record.

27
00:04:47,736 --> 00:04:59,256
Assessment includes what you found and how the patient changed, along
with actions and response. Label a working impression as an
impression, and state what you do not yet know.

28
00:04:59,256 --> 00:05:09,216
Finish with a specific request. If instructions are given, read back
important details and clarify ambiguity before acting within your
authorization.

29
00:05:09,216 --> 00:05:21,624
This fictional example teaches message structure, not a treatment
algorithm. Begin: I am a student with my supervising clinician; I am
concerned about deterioration during transport.

30
00:05:21,624 --> 00:05:34,056
Add the onset and relevant background, then report the actual
observations and times. Saying blood pressure is falling should be
supported by the measured trend, not an isolated impression.

31
00:05:34,056 --> 00:05:44,760
Report what the team is already doing and the response. State
unresolved information instead of offering a confident diagnosis that
the assessment does not support.

32
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:56,160
Request the assistance or decision needed. Urgent reassessment and
necessary care continue while help is sought; a perfect SBAR must not
delay protection of the patient.

33
00:05:56,160 --> 00:06:08,520
MIST organizes a concise trauma summary. Describe the mechanism
without making it a substitute for examination: a concerning mechanism
raises suspicion but does not prove a specific injury.

34
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:18,408
Distinguish an observed injury from a suspected one. Report the
relevant signs and their direction of change, including mental status
and measurements.

35
00:06:18,408 --> 00:06:29,784
For treatment, pair the intervention with timing and response. Saying
oxygen given or bleeding treated leaves out whether the problem
improved, persisted or worsened.

36
00:06:29,784 --> 00:06:41,304
In a formal transfer using the current BCEHS framework, add the other
ATMIST AMBO elements. The same essential clinical facts should survive
a change in the report structure.

37
00:06:41,304 --> 00:06:53,520
ATMIST starts with age and patient identification, then the time of
injury or symptom onset. Identify an uncertain onset as uncertain
rather than replacing it with the time of the call.

38
00:06:53,520 --> 00:07:01,680
M can describe the mechanism of injury or the medical complaint. This
is why the framework is not limited to trauma patients.

39
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:16,656
I includes injuries and relevant examination findings. S gives the
current signs and important changes. Report a measured mental-status
assessment accurately; a greeting alone does not establish a full
neurological score.

40
00:07:16,656 --> 00:07:25,464
The final T connects treatment to response. A receiving clinician
needs to know both what has been tried and whether the patient changed
afterwards.

41
00:07:25,464 --> 00:07:36,696
After the immediate picture, AMBO supplies relevant background. An
allergy entry is more useful when it includes the reported reaction,
not just a drug name.

42
00:07:36,696 --> 00:07:48,144
Unknown allergy status is different from no known allergies.
Similarly, a medication history that was not obtained should not be
presented as a patient taking no medicines.

43
00:07:48,144 --> 00:07:59,400
Include medical, social or family background when it changes care,
safety or interpretation. The complete life history is not required in
the initial focused handover.

44
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:11,760
Other relevant information can include a communication support need or
an unresolved risk. Finish by making it easy for the receiver to ask
questions and identify what still needs checking.

45
00:08:11,760 --> 00:08:24,024
Begin with age, identity and onset: a 62-year-old patient with
breathlessness beginning at thirteen twenty. State the main concern
without claiming an unconfirmed cause.

46
00:08:24,024 --> 00:08:36,432
Report the examination and change: respiratory rate rose from twenty-
two to thirty, and the patient now speaks in shorter phrases. Add the
actual observation times when giving the report.

47
00:08:36,432 --> 00:08:49,392
Then state the care delivered and its response, followed by relevant
allergies, medicines and background. If a medication list or allergy
history is unavailable, identify that gap clearly.

48
00:08:49,392 --> 00:09:00,888
The receiver should hear a changing clinical picture, not just a
string of normal and abnormal numbers. Confirm who has accepted the
report and which concerns need immediate attention.

49
00:09:00,888 --> 00:09:12,864
Use the full drug name, dose with units, route, time and response.
These five facts help the receiving team avoid duplicate dosing and
understand what remains untreated.

50
00:09:12,864 --> 00:09:23,712
Milligrams and micrograms are not interchangeable. State the unit
aloud and keep it attached to the number in the record. Avoid
abbreviations that could be misheard.

51
00:09:23,712 --> 00:09:37,080
Time is the administration time, not merely when the medication was
prepared or the report written. Report repeated doses separately or
with an accurate cumulative total and individual times as relevant.

52
00:09:37,080 --> 00:09:47,040
If a fact is uncertain, say so and verify it. Do not fill in an
assumed dose or response because it seems usual for that medicine.

53
00:09:47,040 --> 00:09:56,136
This supplied fictional record is only for reporting practice. It is
not an instruction to administer this medication or a guide to dose
selection.

54
00:09:56,136 --> 00:10:10,128
A clear report is: ondansetron, four milligrams intravenously, at
fourteen oh eight. At fourteen fifteen, the patient reported less
nausea; no further vomiting was observed during that interval.

55
00:10:10,128 --> 00:10:20,616
Notice that the response is attributed and time-limited. The report
does not claim the underlying illness has resolved or that every
possible adverse effect was excluded.

56
00:10:20,616 --> 00:10:32,496
If another dose was mentioned but cannot be confirmed, state that
uncertainty and seek the record or administering clinician. Guessing
would create a false basis for the next treatment decision.

57
00:10:32,496 --> 00:10:44,472
Closed-loop communication checks that a message was received as
intended. It is particularly useful for critical details, requests and
instructions where a misunderstanding could change care.

58
00:10:44,472 --> 00:10:54,864
The receiver repeats the important content, and the sender confirms or
corrects it. A nod or an indistinct reply may not establish shared
understanding.

59
00:10:54,864 --> 00:11:13,296
AET also lists ISBARR: identification, situation, background,
assessment, recommendation and read-back. Understand the added
identification and confirmation steps without treating this glossary
term as a replacement for current service transfer guidance.

60
00:11:13,296 --> 00:11:24,648
Questions are a safety step, not evidence that the report failed. If a
question exposes missing information, identify whether it can be
checked now and who will follow it up.

61
00:11:24,648 --> 00:11:36,576
Before a handover, identify the appropriate receiving person and gain
their attention. Reduce interruptions when possible and avoid
broadcasting private information to unnecessary listeners.

62
00:11:36,576 --> 00:11:47,016
BCEHS guidance describes a brief attention period for critical
handovers when the patient's condition allows. It must not interrupt
immediately necessary care.

63
00:11:47,016 --> 00:11:59,208
Keep the report focused, then allow questions and clarify unresolved
risks. Time targets in guidance support communication; they are not a
reason to omit a decisive finding.

64
00:11:59,208 --> 00:12:12,192
A bed location or completed radio patch does not by itself prove that
responsibility has transferred. Follow the service process to confirm
acceptance, document the transfer and preserve continuity.

65
00:12:12,192 --> 00:12:23,616
Pause and give a report aloud. You have a mechanism and a symptom, but
not a confirmed fracture, a complete neurological assessment or the
actual observation values.

66
00:12:23,616 --> 00:12:34,272
Do not invent the missing findings. Say what the assessment has
established, obtain the latest measurements and distinguish a
suspected injury from an observed finding.

67
00:12:34,272 --> 00:12:48,072
Use the selected framework to order the information. Include the
timing, symptom, examination, verified trend, actual care and
response, then relevant background and any remaining gap.

68
00:12:48,072 --> 00:12:58,776
Ask a partner to tell you the main concern and what happens next. If
they cannot, revise the emphasis or request rather than merely
speaking faster.
